From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 22:39:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] Ask NVENC for NV12, the layout it encodes from

<details><summary>Claude's draft</summary>

NVENC's native input surface is NV12. yuv420p was accepted -- nvenc.c's
nvenc_map_buffer_format maps AV_PIX_FMT_YUV420P to NV_ENC_BUFFER_FORMAT_YV12
and plane-copies into the mapped surface, swapping U and V -- but the driver
then converts YV12 to NV12 on the device before the encoder sees it.
Requesting NV12 hands it the layout it was going to produce anyway.

Applied to both h264_nvenc and hevc_nvenc: nvenc_hevc.c declares
CODEC_PIXFMTS_ARRAY(ff_nvenc_pix_fmts), the same list h264 uses, so the
argument is identical for the two.

This also harmonises the encoder tables. h264_qsv and hevc_qsv already ask for
NV12 and accept nothing else (qsvenc_h264.c:202). With QSV and NVENC both on
NV12, a producer that can emit NV12 directly -- MCAM Studio's canvas now does,
via the SIMD kernels in python-owl !10765 -- reaches either without PyAV
reformatting the frame on the way in.

Four encoders are deliberately left on yuv420p, for two different reasons.

libopenh264 and libx265 cannot take NV12 at all: libopenh264enc.c:439 declares
only YUV420P/YUVJ420P, and libx265.c has no AV_PIX_FMT_NV12 in any of its csp
tables. NV12 there would introduce a swscale, not remove one. libx264 accepts
NV12 but x264 is planar internally, so there is nothing to gain.

VideoToolbox is the interesting one, and it does not need NV12 either.
yuv420p is a first-class CVPixelBuffer format there, not something routed
through NV12: hwcontext_videotoolbox.c's cv_pix_fmts maps AV_PIX_FMT_YUV420P
to kCVPixelFormatType_420YpCbCr8Planar, and to ...PlanarFullRange when the
frame declares full range. copy_avframe_to_pixel_buffer (videotoolboxenc.c:2333)
then does a plane-for-plane av_image_copy2 into a buffer whose format was
chosen to match the frame, and asserts the plane counts agree. Both formats
are a direct copy; neither converts. So hevc_videotoolbox and
h264_videotoolbox stay as they are.

Measured on an RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell at 1920x1080, all four combinations of
context and frame format land within noise (1.72-1.77 ms/frame), so this is
not a measured speed-up on that card -- it is the encoder being handed what it
asked for, and the precondition for the canvas emitting NV12 without a
reformat. Verified that both hevc_nvenc and h264_nvenc encode an NV12 frame
(808252 and 675856 bytes over 20 frames). The h264_qsv case, where the
conversion is mandatory rather than internal, could not be measured here.

Pre-existing unrelated failure in
tests/test_h264.py::test_encoder_target_bitrate (the fork's per-encoder
default bitrate vs upstream's assertion) is untouched; it fails identically
without this change. There is no tests/test_hevc.py.

